  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   - Central Idea: Instead of relying solely on continuous optimization, this approach combines gradient-based and discrete optimization methods to explore both smooth and structured solutions. The key insight is that optimal functions in minimum overlap problems often exhibit structured patterns, so we represent functions using randomized truncated Fourier series to capture spatial frequencies while maintaining the integral constraint. This reduces dimensionality and allows us to leverage both gradient information and combinatorial search.\n   - Bottleneck Addressed: The previous approach struggles with local optima in high-dimensional spaces. The new method injects structured candidates through Fourier patterns and performs interval analysis to ensure improved feasibility, addressing issues with clip-and-project heuristics.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   - Representation: Use randomized Fourier representation \\( h(x) = \\sum_{k=-K}^{K} a_k \\sin(2\\pi kx) + b_k \\cos(2\\pi kx) + c \\) where coefficients \\((a_k, b_k, c)\\) are initialized randomly, ensuring \\(\\int_0^2 h(x)dx=1\\). Truncate to \\(K = \\log(n_{points})\\).\n   - Objective: Minimize \\(C_5 = \\max_k \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\\). The discrete version is used for calculation.\n   - Constraints: \\(0 \\leq h(x) \\leq 1\\) enforced via change-of-variables (sigmoid-log), and integral constraint \\( \\sum_{i} h[i] dx = 1 \\) baked into Fourier initialization.\n   - Variables: Fourier coefficients and a constant for each harmonic. Optimization proceeds in \\(\\mathbb{R}^{3K+1}\\).\n   - Name and Type: Continuous variables with bounds, requiring derivative-based optimization (SLSQP) or Bayesian optimization with derivative-free constraints.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   - Initialization: Start with Fourier modes at level \\(L = \\log_2(n_{points})\\) producing \\(3L+1\\) variables. Apply projection to fix integral constraint.\n   - Core Optimization: Use COBYLA for derivative-free phase (200 function evaluations) then SLSQP for gradient phase (500 iterations). Handle constraints via penalty functions.\n   - Structured Candidates: Inject \\(10\\) random Fourier patterns by minimizing \\(L_2\\) distance to previous solution.\n   - Disaster Recovery: If progress stalls, use stochastic projection: add/clip samples randomly and re-optimize small subsets.\n   - Best Solution: Track best candidate under true objective evaluator at completion.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   - Starting Settings: \\(K = \\lceil \\log_2(n_{points}) \\rceil\\), random seed tied to problem instance.\n   - Variation Triggers: Re-injection every 100 iterations if worst improvement exceeds \\(\\epsilon=0.005\\).\n   - Changes: Fourier magnitude (over \\([0.1,2.0]\\)), number of coefficients (over \\([8,32]\\)), and penalty scaling (\\(10^{-3}\\) to \\(0.1\\)).\n   - Consequence: Increasing K improves structure at cost of dimensionality, larger coefficients find more energetic patterns.\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n   - Estimation: 100 SLSQP iterations ~\\(0.05 \\cdot time_{budget}\\), injection phase adds \\(0.05 \\cdot time_{budget}\\).\n   - Checkpoints: Time left threshold (\\(80\\)sec) triggers injection. Graceful degradation: if time < 50sec, revert to midpoint-initial SLAB optimizer.\n   - Hardware Estimate: 1 CPU, 1300sec budget, \\(5 \\times 10^6\\) objective evaluations planned.\n   - Failure: Detect flat gradients/infeasibility via surrogate bounds. Fallback: Primal-dual interior point on discretized grid.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - Checks: Integral must satisfy \\(| \\sum h dx - 1 | < 0.1\\), evaluation matches `compute_c5`.\n   - Validation: Every 10 iterations, re-optimize a small (20) subsample for sanity.\n   - Return format: `(h_values, best_score, n_points)`\n   - Redispatch rules: If score above 0.381, rerun locally with relaxed Fourier modes.",
